---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig, siteUrl } from '../lib/site';
const schema = { '@type': 'WebPage', name: 'Phone recording compatibility with Callery', url: siteUrl('/phone-compatibility'), description: 'Callery needs original call recordings accessible in a file manager, with unchanged filenames for caller and date recognition. Google Phone sharing is not a supported bulk archive workflow.' };
const patterns = [
  { recorder: 'Samsung-style call files', pattern: 'Call <caller>_YYMMDD_HHMMSS', recognition: 'Caller and local call date/time', note: 'Also recognizes the “Call recording” prefix.' },
  { recorder: 'Xiaomi dialer (MIUI / HyperOS)', pattern: '<call label>@<phone>_YYYYMMDDHHMMSS', recognition: 'Phone number and local call date/time', note: 'Recognized localized call labels are discarded. Evidence is from older MIUI; HyperOS files with this layout use the same parser. Verify the actual dialer and original files.' },
  { recorder: 'Huawei-style call files', pattern: '<name>@<phone>_YYYYMMDDHHMMSS', recognition: 'Name, phone number, and local call date/time', note: 'Phone number takes priority for contact matching.' },
  { recorder: 'ColorOS call files', pattern: '<caller>-YYMMDDHHmm', recognition: 'Caller and local call date/time', note: 'Minute precision. This filename profile does not certify every OPPO, OnePlus, realme, or ODialer setup.' },
  { recorder: 'GrapheneOS call files', pattern: 'CallRecord_YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS_<phone>', recognition: 'Phone number and local call date/time', note: 'Recording availability depends on the installed system.' },
  { recorder: 'LineageOS call files', pattern: '<phone>_YYMMDD_HHMMSS[mmm]', recognition: 'Phone number and local call date/time', note: 'Optional milliseconds are recognized.' },
  { recorder: 'BCR default filenames', pattern: 'YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.mmm±HHMM…', recognition: 'Timestamp with offset; optional caller details', note: 'Custom filename templates need a separate check.' },
  { recorder: 'ASUS sequence exports', pattern: 'Call recording <sequence>', recognition: 'No caller or call date in the filename', note: 'Recognized as a recording name only.' },
  { recorder: 'Oppo voice recorder', pattern: 'RECYYYYMMDDHHMMSS', recognition: 'No inferred call start date', note: 'Its timestamp describes recording end, not call start.' },
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Phone call recording compatibility" description="Callery requires original recordings accessible in your phone’s file manager. Check Samsung folder copying, conditional OEM dialer support, and Google Phone limitations." schema={schema}>
  <section class="container page-heading"><h1>Start with the<br />original recording files.</h1><p>Callery’s bulk archive workflow requires recordings saved in shared internal storage, visible in your phone’s file manager. Copy the original files with their names intact. A dialer’s recording or Share button alone is not enough.</p></section>
  <section class="container compatibility-checks" aria-label="Three compatibility checks"><article><span>1</span><h2>Record with a compatible dialer</h2><p>Check the OEM dialer route for your exact model, region, full firmware build, and dialer version. Phones from the same brand can need different methods. Some routes use manufacturer settings; others are clearly scoped community reports. Installing another Phone app alone does not establish recording support.</p></article><article><span>2</span><h2>Find the originals in Files</h2><p>You must be able to select and copy recordings together from a normal storage folder. If recordings are only available inside the dialer, that setup does not meet Callery’s archive requirements.</p></article><article><span>3</span><h2>Keep names and check metadata</h2><p>Preserve original filenames: Callery reads recognized caller and call-date details from them. Test a few supported audio files before copying years of recordings.</p></article></section>
  <section class="container section access-section"><div class="section-intro"><h2>Check the dialer.<br />Then check its folder.</h2><p>Check the current setup: <strong>original files accessible</strong> when the folder and metadata checks pass; <strong>OEM setup required</strong> when an applicable dialer route needs completing; or <strong>not established for this build</strong> when no working route is verified. A phone shipped with Google Phone may have an OEM setup path.</p></div><div class="access-list"><article><div><h3>Samsung Phone</h3><span class="pill">Original files accessible</span></div><p>On supported Galaxy recording setups, open My Files → Internal storage → Recordings → Call. Copy that folder or select its original files together, including over USB. Keep every filename unchanged.</p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/find-and-export-samsung-call-recordings">Copy Samsung’s recording folder</a></article><article><div><h3>Google Phone workflow</h3><span class="pill">Not established for this build</span></div><p>Google’s individual sharing route does not meet the original-folder requirements. This describes the current recording setup, not permanent hardware incompatibility. On phones with an applicable OEM dialer route, check that setup for future recordings. Existing private Google Phone recordings do not migrate automatically.</p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/export-google-phone-call-recordings">Why Google Phone does not fit</a></article><article><div><h3>Xiaomi / Redmi / POCO</h3><span class="pill">OEM setup required</span></div><p>Use Xiaomi’s existing OEM dialer or restore verified firmware system packages using the scoped ADB guide. Missing packages or failed restoration mean incompatible current firmware for this route. Keep Google apps installed; no external APKs, root, or firmware changes. Check calling, original-folder access, and filename recognition before importing.</p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/enable-call-recording-xiaomi-hyperos">Check Xiaomi’s dialer and storage</a></article><article><div><h3>OnePlus / OPPO / realme</h3><span class="pill">OEM setup required</span></div><p>Check the built-in OEM dialer first, official ODialer where offered, or a model/build-specific activation report. These are different routes; no brand-wide method or universal code is established. Confirm recording works, find the original folder in File Manager, and check unchanged filenames in Callery.</p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/enable-call-recording-oppo-odialer">Compare the three dialer routes</a></article><article><div><h3>Vivo / iQOO</h3><span class="pill">OEM setup required</span></div><p>Some firmware offers an OEM Phone &amp; Contacts app. Verify that its recordings appear as original files in storage. Opening audio only in Recorder is insufficient, and automatic Vivo/iQOO filename recognition has not been verified.</p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/enable-call-recording-vivo-iqoo">Check Vivo and iQOO requirements</a></article></div><p class="note sources-note">Sources reviewed November 12, 2026: <a href="https://www.samsungsvc.co.kr/solution/39738">Samsung’s recording folders</a>, <a href="https://www.samsungsvc.co.kr/solution/38168">Samsung’s USB copying instructions</a>, <a href="https://support.google.com/phoneapp/answer/9803950">Google’s individual-call sharing instructions</a>, and <a href={`${siteConfig.repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/recording-filenames.md`}>Callery’s filename reference</a>. The linked device guides give additional model and software limits. No hardware certification is implied.</p></section>
  <section id="accepted-formats" class="formats-section"><div class="container split section-tight"><div><h2>The file is the starting point.</h2><p>Callery accepts these upload formats. Confirm the extension of your actual recording, then try a few files before transferring an entire archive.</p></div><div><div class="format-list"><span>MP3</span><span>WAV</span><span>M4A</span><span>MP4</span><span>WebM</span></div><p class="note">AMR, standalone AAC, and OGG/Opus are not currently accepted. A recognized filename does not add support for an unsupported audio format.</p></div></div></section>
  <section id="filename-patterns" class="container section"><div class="section-intro"><h2>What Callery can<br />read from a filename.</h2><p>These naming layouts describe files, not certified devices. The uploader can accept an unfamiliar filename, but the result has an unknown caller and upload time. That fallback does not provide the contact-and-date archive workflow described here.</p></div><div class="pattern-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Recognized recording filename patterns"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Recorder profile</th><th scope="col">Filename layout</th><th scope="col">Automatic metadata</th></tr></thead><tbody>{patterns.map(item => <tr><th scope="row">{item.recorder}</th><td><code>{item.pattern}</code><small>{item.note}</small></td><td>{item.recognition}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p class="note pattern-note">Dates without a UTC offset do not identify the phone’s timezone. Review dates and contact matches after a small test import. Copying a file must preserve its original name; a changed filesystem modification date cannot restore missing call metadata.</p></section>
  <section class="closing"><div class="container"><div><h2>Start with a copy.<br />Keep your originals.</h2><p>Our backup guide walks through a careful first import.</p></div><a href="/guides/backup-and-organize-android-call-recordings" class="button">Read the Android backup guide</a></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
